didates. We should consider the criteria. We can either agree with B or propose another candidate. Let's evaluate.\n\nCandidate B strengths: very conscientious (reliability), handles stress well (stress resilience), good at assessing weather (technical), excellent computer skills (technical). Concerns: weak memory for numbers (attention), makes nasty remarks about colleagues (crew cooperation, communication). Also regarded as pretentious, sometimes adopts wrong tone.\n\nCandidate A strengths: anticipate dangerous situations, see complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership qualities. Concerns: unfriendly, eats unhealthily. Unfriendly may affect crew cooperation, communication. Eating unhealthily maybe health but not directly criteria.\n\nCandidate C strengths: can make correct decisions quickly (decision quality), puts concern for others above everything (crew cooperation maybe), excellent attention skills. Concerns: difficulty communicating ideas, regarded as egocentric, not willing to further education (adaptability). Also maybe stress resilience unknown.\n\nCandidate D strengths: responds to unexpected events adequately (stress resilience), can concentrate very well (attention), solves problems extremely well (decision quality/technical), takes responsibility seriously (responsibility). Concerns: considered moody, regarded as a loner (crew cooperation), maybe hot temper.\n\nWe need to pick best overall fit. Candidate B has strong reliability and stress, but interpersonal issues. Candidate D has strong problem solving, responsibility, concentration, but moody/loner. Candidate C has decision quality and attention, but communication issues and egocentric. Candidate A has anticipation and leadership but unfriendly.\n\nWhich is best? The panel likely values crew cooperation and communication heavily for safety. Interpersonal issues are serious. Candidate D's moody/loner may also be serious. Candidate C's egocentric and communication difficulty also serious. Candidate A's unfriendly also serious. All have issues.\n\nWe need to decide which candidate's strengths outweigh concerns. Perhaps Candidate D's responsibility and problem solving are crucial for long-distance flights, and moody/loner could be mitigated. But moody could affect stress handling. However D responds adequately to unexpected events, indicating stress resilience. So D seems balanced: stress, attention, problem solving, responsibility. Concerns: moody, loner (crew cooperation). Could be mitigated with team building.\n\nCandidate B's interpersonal issues are nasty remarks, which could be dangerous. Might be less mitigable.\n\nThus I might argue for Candidate D as better overall fit. Use evidence: responds to unexpected events adequately (stress), concentrates well (attention), solves problems extremely well (technical/decision), takes responsibility seriously (responsibility). Concerns: moody, loner (crew cooperation). Compare to B's nasty remarks.\n\nThus I will propose Candidate D as my provisional recommendation, challenging Agent 2's recommendation.\n\nWe need to reference Agent 2's point. Provide evidence from our notes. Provide implication.\n\nNow produce PUBLIC_MESSAGE and METADATA_JSON with vote \"Candidate D\"."}
